{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Use a real sanitizer library, and remember it isn't a one-time fix</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Don't hand-roll a sanitizer with regular expressions for real production code, matching every dangerous HTML shape with regex is famous for missing edge cases (case variations, encoded characters, nested tags), which is exactly why DOMPurify exists as a maintained, widely-audited library instead of a snippet everyone reinvents slightly wrong.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Sanitization needs to stay current: DOMPurify ships updates specifically because new mutation-XSS techniques and browser parsing quirks keep getting discovered, pin a real version and keep it updated rather than vendoring a snapshot. Also add a Content-Security-Policy header as a second, independent layer, if a sanitizer bug ever lets something through, a strict <code>script-src</code> can still block it from executing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This applies regardless of which model or provider you're using, and regardless of how much you trust that provider's alignment work, the vulnerable step is entirely on your own rendering path, not inside the model. Prompt injection (text hidden in a document the model reads, or in this case a customer's own pasted content) can steer what a model reproduces even when the model itself is behaving exactly as designed; the only reliable boundary is treating every string that reaches your DOM through a markdown-to-HTML-to-innerHTML pipeline as untrusted, the same way you'd treat a raw form field, no matter how many systems it passed through first.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

